 *   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate camera poses and calibration; performance depends on the quality of the shared CNN feature extractor [S1].
 *   **When to use:** When generalizable semantic rendering is required with limited computational resources (GPU memory/FLOPs) [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When extremely high precision in view-dependent effects is needed and computational budget is unlimited (dense attention might be slightly better, though CRA is comparable) [S1].
 
 ## Background
 *   **Origin:** Proposed in the S-Ray method [S1].
-*   **History:** S-Ray was presented at CVPR 2023 [S1]. It was developed to address the insufficiency of single-point feature extraction for semantic prediction in NeRF-based generalization rendering methods [S1].
+*   **History:** Developed to address the insufficiency of single-point feature extraction for semantic prediction in NeRF-based generalization rendering methods [S1].
 *   **Usage:** Used in the S-Ray method for generalizable semantic rendering, compared against MVSNeRF, NeuRay, and Semantic-NeRF [S1]. It is designed to work without positional encoding engineering, unlike GPNR [S1].
 
